# Step 7 — Initialize Git and connect to GitHub

Once the folders and README are created, run these commands in your VS Code terminal:

```bash
git init
git add .
git commit -m "Initial PathSnap repository structure"
```

Then create the GitHub repository named `PathSnap`, and connect it:

```bash
git branch -M main
git remote add origin https://github.com/YOUR-USERNAME/PathSnap.git
git push -u origin main
```

Replace `YOUR-USERNAME` with your GitHub username.

---

# Step 8 — Your Laptop 2 architecture

Since you're Member 4, you're responsible for the **second laptop (UI)** connecting to the **first laptop (ML server)**.

<box border radius="xl" padding={3} gap={1}>
  <box background="#DCEBFF" theme="light" radius="md" padding={3} align="center" gap={1}>
    **Laptop 2 — YOUR WORK**

    <icon name="monitor" size="xl"/>

    **PathSnap UI**

    Trip selector • Map • Results • Metrics
  </box>
  <text textAlign="center">↓ HTTP request over Wi-Fi / LAN</text>
  <box background="#D9F0E3" theme="light" radius="md" padding={3} align="center" gap={1}>
    **Laptop 1 — ML Server**

    <icon name="server" size="xl"/>

    **FastAPI**

    Data → Candidate Roads → ML → HMM/Viterbi → Results
  </box>
</box>

Your UI must not independently implement the ML logic. It should call the backend API, receive the output and visualize it.

Eventually, the UI will send something like:

```json
{
  "trip_id": "00000000"
}
```

And the backend will return the route coordinates, predictions, and evaluation metrics that are available and permitted for that trip.

